2026-10-29|Minimum Cost to Merge Files

Question:
Given an integer array files[], find the minimum total cost required to merge all files into a single file. Merging files of sizes x and y costs x + y, and produces a new file of size x + y.

Approach:
1. Insert all file sizes into a min-heap, which always provides the smallest file size.
2. While more than one file remains:
   - Remove the two smallest file sizes, x and y.
   - Merge them to create a new file of size x + y.
   - Add x + y to the total computation cost.
   - Insert the merged file back into the min-heap.
3. When only one file remains, return the accumulated cost.
4. If there is only one file initially, no merge is needed, so the cost is 0.

Solution:
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int minCost(vector<int>& files) {
        priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> minHeap;

        for (int size : files) {
            minHeap.push(size);
        }

        int totalCost = 0;

        while (minHeap.size() > 1) {
            int first = minHeap.top();
            minHeap.pop();

            int second = minHeap.top();
            minHeap.pop();

            int mergedSize = first + second;
            totalCost += mergedSize;

            minHeap.push(mergedSize);
        }

        return totalCost;
    }
};

Time Complexity: O(n log n), because each merge performs heap operations, and there are n - 1 merges.

Space Complexity: O(n), for storing the file sizes in the min-heap.
